/* Original educational summaries. Thresholds are anchors, not complete criteria. */
const diagnosisReview={
adhd:{clinical:{course:"Several symptoms begin before age 12. The pattern lasts at least six months, occurs in two or more settings, and exceeds developmental expectations.",impact:"Symptoms interfere with or reduce the quality of social, academic or occupational functioning.",exclusions:"Consider sleep, anxiety, depression, trauma, learning needs, sensory access, substances and medical causes. A new adult-onset complaint requires investigation.",cooccur:"Autism, learning disorders, anxiety, depression and substance use disorders may coexist."},plain:{course:"Some symptoms must have started before age 12. They last at least six months, appear in at least two settings, and go beyond what is expected for the person's age.",impact:"The difficulties get in the way of relationships, learning, work or daily tasks.",exclusions:"Poor sleep, worry, depression, trauma, learning needs, barriers to access, substances and health problems can also affect attention. New attention problems in adulthood need a careful history.",cooccur:"A person may also have autism, learning difficulties, anxiety, depression or problems with substance use."}},
asd:{clinical:{course:"Features arise in early development, though demands or masking may delay recognition. Establish both social-communication and restricted/repetitive domains.",impact:"Assess clinically significant impact in current functioning, including the effort and supports required for participation.",exclusions:"Differentiate language or intellectual disability, sensory impairment, social anxiety and cultural communication differences. Introversion alone is insufficient.",cooccur:"ADHD, anxiety, intellectual or language difficulties and sleep problems may coexist."},plain:{course:"The pattern starts early in life, even if it is recognized later. Ask about both communication and relationships, and repeated patterns, routines, interests or sensory experiences.",impact:"Ask what is difficult in daily life, what support helps, and how much effort participation takes.",exclusions:"Language, learning, hearing or vision needs, fear of judgment and cultural differences can also affect communication. Being quiet or introverted alone does not mean autism.",cooccur:"ADHD, anxiety, learning or language difficulties and sleep problems can also be present."}},
gad:{clinical:{course:"Difficult-to-control worry across domains occurs more days than not for at least six months. Associated symptom requirements differ for children and adults.",impact:"Anxiety causes clinically significant distress or interference with daily functioning.",exclusions:"Assess thyroid disease, stimulants, withdrawal and other medical or substance effects; distinguish trauma-linked symptoms, obsessions and focused fears.",cooccur:"Depression, other anxiety disorders, ADHD and substance use disorders may coexist."},plain:{course:"Worry about several parts of life is hard to control and happens on most days for at least six months. The required number of other symptoms differs for children and adults.",impact:"The worry causes substantial distress or gets in the way of everyday life.",exclusions:"Thyroid problems, stimulants, withdrawal and other health or substance effects can cause anxiety. Ask whether thoughts are tied to trauma, rituals or one particular fear.",cooccur:"Depression, other anxiety problems, ADHD or problems with substance use can also be present."}},
social:{clinical:{course:"Fear of scrutiny, avoidance or distressed endurance typically persists for six months or longer. In children, assess peer situations, not only interactions with adults.",impact:"Fear limits desired social, educational or occupational participation or causes clinically significant distress.",exclusions:"Assess actual threats, discrimination, cultural expectations, communication differences, substances and medical contributors.",cooccur:"Autism, depression and other anxiety disorders may coexist; distinguish the source of each difficulty."},plain:{course:"Fear of being judged, avoidance or staying in situations with great distress usually lasts at least six months. For children, ask about being with other children as well as adults.",impact:"Fear gets in the way of wanted relationships, learning or work, or causes substantial distress.",exclusions:"Ask about real danger, discrimination, cultural expectations, communication needs, substances and health problems.",cooccur:"Autism, depression or other anxiety conditions may also be present. Ask what is driving each difficulty."}},
panic:{clinical:{course:"Recurrent unexpected attacks are followed by at least one month of persistent concern about further attacks or their consequences, or maladaptive behavioral change.",impact:"Assess avoidance, emergency visits, disruption of routines and distress related to feared recurrence.",exclusions:"Rule out relevant cardiopulmonary, endocrine, medication and substance effects. Panic attacks in another disorder do not automatically establish panic disorder.",cooccur:"Agoraphobia, depression and other anxiety disorders may coexist; evaluate agoraphobia separately."},plain:{course:"Repeated panic attacks happen unexpectedly. At least one attack is followed by a month or more of continuing worry about further attacks or changes in behavior that make life harder.",impact:"Ask whether fear of another attack changes routines, leads to avoidance or repeated emergency visits, or causes distress.",exclusions:"Heart, lung or hormone problems, medicines and substances may cause similar symptoms. Panic attacks can occur in other conditions without panic disorder.",cooccur:"Fear of places where escape or help feels difficult, depression and other anxiety conditions may also be present."}},
mdd:{clinical:{course:"A major depressive episode lasts at least two weeks, includes depressed mood or loss of interest plus additional symptoms, and differs from baseline. Assess lifetime mania and hypomania.",impact:"Symptoms cause clinically significant distress or impairment. Assess suicide risk directly when clinically indicated.",exclusions:"Consider bipolar course, substances, medications, medical illness and grief. Bereavement does not automatically exclude a depressive episode.",cooccur:"Anxiety, trauma-related conditions and substance use disorders may coexist. Chronic depression requires longitudinal assessment."},plain:{course:"An episode lasts at least two weeks. Low mood or loss of interest occurs with other changes and differs from the person's usual state. Ask about past periods of unusually high energy and mood.",impact:"Symptoms cause substantial distress or affect everyday functioning. Ask directly about suicide risk when the situation calls for it.",exclusions:"Check for bipolar episodes, substances, medicines, health problems and grief. A person who is grieving can also have a depressive episode.",cooccur:"Anxiety, trauma-related difficulties or problems with substance use may also be present. A long history of depression needs its own timeline."}},
pdd:{clinical:{course:"Depressed mood is present most of the day, more days than not, for at least two years in adults or one year in youth; youth may have irritable mood. Symptom-free intervals do not exceed two months.",impact:"Assess cumulative effects on motivation, relationships, work and self-care, not only acute worsening.",exclusions:"Assess lifetime mania/hypomania, cyclothymic course, substances and medical causes. Chronic unhappiness alone is insufficient.",cooccur:"Full major depressive episodes can occur within the persistent course; anxiety and substance problems may also coexist."},plain:{course:"Low mood occurs for much of the day on most days for at least two years in adults or one year in children and teens. Young people may be irritable. Breaks without symptoms last no more than two months.",impact:"Ask how years of low mood affect motivation, relationships, work and self-care, as well as periods of worsening.",exclusions:"Ask about past high-energy mood episodes, long-term mood shifts, substances and health problems. Longstanding unhappiness alone is not enough.",cooccur:"A person can have full depressive episodes within this longer pattern, and may also have anxiety or substance problems."}},
bipolar1:{clinical:{course:"Mania usually lasts at least one week, or any duration if hospitalization is necessary. It entails marked impairment, hospitalization or psychosis. Major depression is not required.",impact:"Assess safety, judgment, financial or sexual risk, sleep need and substantial functional change. Urgent mania or psychosis warrants prompt assessment.",exclusions:"Evaluate substances, medications and medical causes; chart whether psychosis occurs outside mood episodes.",cooccur:"Anxiety, ADHD and substance use disorders may coexist. Personality patterns require assessment outside acute episodes."},plain:{course:"Mania usually lasts at least one week, or less if hospital care is needed. It causes major disruption, requires hospital care, or includes loss of contact with reality. Depression is not required for bipolar I.",impact:"Ask about safety, judgment, risky spending or sexual behavior, need for sleep and major changes in daily life. Mania or loss of contact with reality may need prompt care.",exclusions:"Substances, medicines and health problems can cause similar changes. Ask whether experiences such as voices or fixed false beliefs happen outside mood episodes.",cooccur:"Anxiety, ADHD or problems with substance use may also be present. Assess longstanding personality patterns when an acute episode has settled."}},
bipolar2:{clinical:{course:"Requires major depression and hypomania, with no mania. Hypomania lasts at least four consecutive days, is observable, and lacks marked impairment, hospitalization or psychosis.",impact:"Depression and the course as a whole may be severely impairing. Assess suicide risk and mixed symptoms; bipolar II is not inherently mild.",exclusions:"Assess substances, medications, medical causes, ADHD and interpersonal reactivity. Psychosis in an elevated episode indicates mania, not hypomania.",cooccur:"Anxiety, ADHD, substance use and personality disorders may coexist when independently supported."},plain:{course:"Bipolar II includes depressive episodes and at least four days in a row of a noticeable high-energy mood change called hypomania, with no past mania. Hypomania does not cause major impairment, need hospital care, or involve loss of contact with reality.",impact:"Depression and the overall pattern can seriously affect life. Ask about suicide risk and combinations of depressive and high-energy symptoms. Bipolar II can be severe.",exclusions:"Check substances, medicines, health problems, ADHD and reactions to relationship events. Loss of contact with reality during a high-energy mood episode points to mania.",cooccur:"Anxiety, ADHD, substance problems or a personality disorder can also be present when each is supported by its own evidence."}},
ptsd:{clinical:{course:"Qualifying trauma is followed by the required symptom clusters for more than one month. Use developmentally adapted criteria for children six and younger; delayed expression is possible.",impact:"Assess distress, avoidance, relationships, concentration, sleep, functioning, dissociation and safety without requiring unnecessary trauma detail.",exclusions:"Adversity alone does not establish qualifying exposure. Assess acute stress disorder, adjustment, mood/anxiety disorders, substances and medical causes.",cooccur:"Depression, anxiety, dissociative symptoms and substance use problems may coexist."},plain:{course:"After an event that meets the trauma requirement, the required pattern of symptoms lasts more than one month. Criteria are adapted for children age six and younger. The full pattern may appear later.",impact:"Ask about distress, avoidance, relationships, concentration, sleep, daily life, feeling disconnected and safety. Detailed retelling is not needed to establish every assessment point.",exclusions:"Not every difficult event meets the trauma requirement. Consider early stress reactions, adjustment difficulties, depression, anxiety, substances and health problems.",cooccur:"Depression, anxiety, feeling disconnected and substance problems can also be present."}},
ocd:{clinical:{course:"No minimum month-based duration is specified. Obsessions and/or compulsions are time-consuming, typically more than one hour daily, or cause significant distress or impairment.",impact:"Assess mental rituals, avoidance, reassurance seeking, time lost and interference; insight ranges from good to absent.",exclusions:"Distinguish general worry, psychosis, repetitive developmental patterns and OCPD; assess medication, substance and medical effects.",cooccur:"OCPD, anxiety, depression and tic disorders may coexist. Intrusive harm thoughts require assessment of meaning and actual intent, not automatic inference of dangerousness."},plain:{course:"There is no required number of months. Thoughts or rituals take substantial time, often more than an hour a day, or cause substantial distress or problems in life.",impact:"Ask about repeated mental steps, avoidance, asking others for reassurance, lost time and daily difficulties. People vary in how clearly they recognize the problem.",exclusions:"Ask whether the pattern is broad worry, loss of contact with reality, a developmental repeated behavior or a longstanding need for control. Medicines, substances and health problems matter too.",cooccur:"A person may also have OCPD, anxiety, depression or tics. An upsetting thought about harm does not by itself mean the person wants to act on it; ask about meaning and intent."}},
ocpd:{clinical:{course:"An enduring, pervasive pattern is evident by early adulthood. Assess stability across contexts and over time rather than preferences in one situation.",impact:"Perfectionism and control compromise flexibility, efficiency, task completion or relationships.",exclusions:"Distinguish conscientiousness, cultural or occupational norms, OCD and responses to instability. Apply general personality-disorder requirements.",cooccur:"OCD, anxiety and depression may coexist. The person may experience standards as reasonable, but insight and distress vary."},plain:{course:"The broad, lasting pattern is present by early adulthood and appears in different parts of life. One preference or difficult situation is not enough.",impact:"Strict standards and control make it harder to adapt, finish tasks efficiently or maintain relationships.",exclusions:"Carefulness, cultural or work expectations, OCD and reactions to unstable conditions can look similar. A personality diagnosis requires a wider assessment.",cooccur:"OCD, anxiety or depression may also be present. A person may see their rules as sensible, but awareness and distress vary."}},
bpd:{clinical:{course:"An enduring pattern involving relationships, identity and affect is assessed across contexts, with onset by early adulthood. In adolescents, assess developmental fit and persistence carefully.",impact:"Assess interpersonal and functional costs, dissociation, impulsivity and self-harm or suicide risk individually; no single behavior is diagnostic.",exclusions:"Differentiate mood episodes, PTSD, ADHD, autism, substances and situational crisis. Trauma history is neither required nor sufficient.",cooccur:"PTSD, mood, anxiety, eating and substance use disorders may coexist. Avoid inferring motives or a fixed prognosis."},plain:{course:"A lasting pattern in relationships, sense of self and emotions appears across settings, starting by early adulthood. For teens, carefully consider age, development and how long it has lasted.",impact:"Ask about daily difficulties, relationships, feeling disconnected, impulsive actions and any self-harm or suicide risk. One behavior cannot establish the diagnosis.",exclusions:"Mood episodes, trauma-related conditions, ADHD, autism, substances and a current crisis may look similar. Trauma history alone neither establishes nor rules out BPD.",cooccur:"Trauma-related, mood, anxiety, eating or substance conditions can also occur. Do not assume someone's motives or that improvement is impossible."}},
schizo:{clinical:{course:"Continuous signs persist at least six months, including an active phase of about one month, or less if successfully treated. Active symptoms and functional decline are both assessed.",impact:"Assess change in work, relationships or self-care, negative symptoms, cognition, support needs and immediate safety. Early psychosis needs timely evaluation.",exclusions:"Assess delirium, neurologic or other medical illness, substances, mood-related psychosis and cultural meaning. Consider brief psychotic, schizophreniform and schizoaffective disorders.",cooccur:"Depression, anxiety and substance use disorders may coexist. In schizophrenia, major mood episodes, if present, occupy a minority of the total active/residual illness."},plain:{course:"Signs continue for at least six months, including about a month of active symptoms, or less if treatment works sooner. Assess both the symptom pattern and a decline in everyday functioning.",impact:"Ask about work, relationships, self-care, reduced motivation or expression, thinking, support and safety. New psychotic symptoms need timely assessment.",exclusions:"Check sudden confusion, brain or other health problems, substances, mood episodes and cultural meaning. Other psychotic disorders differ in duration and in how mood episodes relate to psychosis.",cooccur:"Depression, anxiety or substance problems may also occur. For schizophrenia, full mood episodes, if present, take up less than half of the total time when psychotic symptoms are strong or still partly present."}},
adjustment:{clinical:{pattern:"Emotional or behavioral symptoms follow an identifiable stressor, with disproportionate distress in context and/or significant impairment.",course:"Onset is within three months of the stressor. After the stressor and its consequences end, symptoms do not persist beyond six additional months.",impact:"Assess culturally contextualized distress and interference with relationships, work, school or other important activities.",exclusions:"Do not use when another disorder better accounts for symptoms, for an exacerbation of an existing disorder, or for a culturally expected grief response.",cooccur:"Other conditions may be present, but symptoms assigned to adjustment disorder need a distinct explanation. Assess safety even when the stressor seems understandable."},plain:{pattern:"After a particular stressor, emotional or behavioral difficulties cause more distress than expected in context, or interfere substantially with life, or both.",course:"Difficulties start within three months. They do not continue more than six months after the stressor and its effects have ended.",impact:"Ask about culture and circumstances, as well as effects on relationships, work, school or other important activities.",exclusions:"Use another explanation if it better fits, if an existing condition has worsened, or if the response is expected grief in that culture.",cooccur:"Other conditions may also be present, but adjustment difficulties need their own explanation. An understandable stressor does not remove the need to assess safety."}},
insomnia:{clinical:{course:"Sleep difficulty occurs at least three nights weekly for at least three months despite adequate opportunity, with significant daytime distress or impairment.",impact:"Assess daytime alertness, cognition, mood, occupational or driving safety and opportunity for restorative sleep.",exclusions:"Assess other sleep disorders, circadian mismatch, substances, medications and medical conditions. Comorbid illness does not automatically exclude independently significant insomnia.",cooccur:"Mood, anxiety, pain and substance problems may coexist and interact bidirectionally with sleep."},plain:{course:"Sleep problems happen at least three nights a week for at least three months, despite enough opportunity to sleep, and cause substantial daytime distress or difficulties.",impact:"Ask about alertness, thinking, mood, safety at work or while driving, and having a real chance to sleep.",exclusions:"Other sleep conditions, a mismatched body clock, substances, medicines and health problems can contribute. Another condition does not automatically rule out insomnia that needs its own care.",cooccur:"Mood, anxiety, pain or substance problems may also be present. They can affect sleep, and poor sleep can affect them."}},
aud:{clinical:{course:"At least two applicable substance-use criteria occur within 12 months. Severity depends on criterion count; intoxication and withdrawal are assessed separately.",impact:"Assess control, role functioning, relationships, hazardous use and health consequences without diagnosing from quantity alone.",exclusions:"A single physiological feature or drinking frequency alone is insufficient. Evaluate other substances, medical illness and the timing of mood or psychotic symptoms.",cooccur:"Other substance, mood, anxiety and trauma-related disorders may coexist. Alcohol withdrawal can be life-threatening; assess medical support before abrupt cessation in a dependent person."},plain:{course:"At least two relevant signs occur within a 12-month period. The number of signs helps describe severity. Being intoxicated or having withdrawal is assessed separately.",impact:"Ask about control over drinking, responsibilities, relationships, unsafe use and health. Amount alone does not settle the diagnosis.",exclusions:"One physical sign or frequent drinking alone is not enough. Ask about other substances, health and when mood or unusual experiences happen.",cooccur:"Other substance, mood, anxiety or trauma-related conditions may also be present. Alcohol withdrawal can be life-threatening; a person who is dependent may need medical help to stop safely."}},
cannabis:{clinical:{course:"At least two applicable substance-use criteria occur within 12 months. Establish impaired control and consequences; use, intoxication and a use disorder are distinct.",impact:"Assess effects on learning, work, relationships, driving, motivation and health, considering dose, potency and route.",exclusions:"Cannabis use alone does not establish CUD. Intoxication or cannabis-induced psychosis can occur without CUD; primary psychosis requires separate assessment.",cooccur:"Alcohol or other substance disorders, anxiety, depression and primary psychotic disorders may coexist."},plain:{course:"At least two relevant signs occur within 12 months. Ask about difficulty controlling use and its consequences. Using cannabis, being intoxicated and having a use disorder are different things.",impact:"Ask about learning, work, relationships, driving, motivation and health, including how much, how strong and how cannabis is used.",exclusions:"Using cannabis alone is not a use disorder. Cannabis-related loss of contact with reality can occur without a use disorder, and a separate psychotic condition also needs assessment.",cooccur:"Alcohol or other substance problems, anxiety, depression or a psychotic disorder can also be present."}},
anorexia:{clinical:{pattern:"Energy restriction leads to significantly low weight, with weight-gain fear or interfering behaviors and disturbance in weight/shape experience, self-evaluation or recognition of seriousness.",course:"No fixed minimum duration. Assess weight trajectory relative to age, development, health and growth, and medical stability promptly.",impact:"Assess nutrition, cardiovascular and metabolic safety, daily functioning and cognitive effects; serious risk is not determined by appearance.",exclusions:"Consider medical weight loss, ARFID and other eating disorders. Atypical anorexia can carry serious risk without significantly low weight.",cooccur:"Anxiety, depression, OCD and other difficulties may coexist. Bingeing or purging can occur within anorexia nervosa."},plain:{pattern:"Eating less than the body needs leads to significantly low weight. There is fear of weight gain or behavior that prevents it, with difficulties in body experience, self-worth or recognizing the seriousness.",course:"There is no fixed minimum length of time. Assess weight and growth over time, age, health and current medical safety promptly.",impact:"Ask about nutrition, heart and body safety, everyday life and thinking. Appearance does not tell you how serious the risk is.",exclusions:"Health problems and other eating conditions can cause restriction or weight loss. A similar pattern without significantly low weight can still be medically serious.",cooccur:"Anxiety, depression, OCD or other difficulties can also be present. Some people with anorexia also binge or purge."}},
bulimia:{clinical:{course:"Binges and inappropriate compensatory behaviors occur, on average, at least weekly for three months; evaluate shape/weight-based self-evaluation and anorexia exclusion.",impact:"Assess electrolyte, cardiac, dental and gastrointestinal risks plus distress, secrecy and functional interference.",exclusions:"Do not diagnose bulimia when the episodes occur exclusively during anorexia nervosa. Differentiate BED, subjective binges and other eating syndromes.",cooccur:"Mood, anxiety and substance problems may coexist. Compensatory behaviors require explicit, nonjudgmental assessment at any body size."},plain:{course:"Binges and harmful attempts to make up for eating occur at least once a week on average for three months. Ask about how weight and shape affect self-worth and whether anorexia better fits.",impact:"Ask about body chemistry, heart, dental or stomach problems, distress, secrecy and daily life.",exclusions:"If the pattern occurs only during anorexia, it is assessed within anorexia. Other eating conditions and feeling out of control without a large binge need careful distinction.",cooccur:"Mood, anxiety or substance problems may also be present. Ask respectfully about vomiting, fasting, laxative misuse or excessive exercise at any body size."}},
bed:{clinical:{course:"Binge episodes occur at least weekly on average for three months, with associated features and marked distress, without regular inappropriate compensatory behavior.",impact:"Assess distress, loss of control and daily-life interference independently of body size.",exclusions:"Differentiate bulimia, anorexia, ordinary overeating and other eating concerns. A binge requires both an unusually large amount in context and loss of control.",cooccur:"Mood, anxiety and other health conditions may coexist. Avoid equating weight with diagnosis or moralizing eating behavior."},plain:{course:"Binges happen at least once a week on average for three months, with other related signs and substantial distress, without regular harmful attempts to make up for eating.",impact:"Ask about distress, feeling unable to stop and effects on daily life. Body size does not establish the diagnosis.",exclusions:"Distinguish other eating conditions and ordinary overeating. A binge involves both an unusually large amount in that situation and feeling unable to control eating.",cooccur:"Mood, anxiety or other health conditions may also be present. Neither weight nor eating behavior tells you someone's character."}},
illness:{clinical:{course:"Illness preoccupation persists at least six months, though the feared illness may change. Somatic symptoms are absent or mild; health anxiety and checking or avoidance are prominent.",impact:"Assess time spent worrying, repeated reassurance or checking, avoidance and impact on care and daily life.",exclusions:"Evaluate relevant medical needs and distinguish SSD, OCD, panic and fixed delusional illness beliefs. Risk factors or illness do not automatically exclude disproportionate anxiety.",cooccur:"Anxiety, depression and medical illness may coexist; provide proportionate medical assessment rather than dismissal."},plain:{course:"Fear of having or getting a serious illness lasts at least six months, even if the feared illness changes. Physical symptoms are absent or mild; worry, checking or avoidance are prominent.",impact:"Ask how much time worry takes, how often reassurance or checking is sought, and whether fear prevents care or everyday activities.",exclusions:"Assess real health needs. Other explanations include distressing physical symptoms, OCD, panic or a fixed false illness belief. A real illness or risk factor does not automatically rule out excessive health anxiety.",cooccur:"Anxiety, depression or a medical condition may also be present. Appropriate health assessment and attention to anxiety can both matter."}},
somatic:{clinical:{course:"The symptom-related state is persistent, typically more than six months; individual symptoms may change. Assess distressing symptoms and excessive related thoughts, feelings or behaviors.",impact:"Assess symptom burden, disproportionate health-related preoccupation and interference while validating the person's physical experience.",exclusions:"Medically unexplained symptoms are not required. Medical disease may coexist; do not infer fabrication or assume a normal test excludes disease.",cooccur:"Anxiety, depression, pain and other medical conditions may coexist."},plain:{course:"The pattern usually lasts more than six months, even if particular symptoms change. Physical symptoms cause distress, with worry or responses that become excessive in context.",impact:"Ask how symptoms and worry affect daily life. Take the physical experience seriously while assessing the response to it.",exclusions:"Symptoms do not have to be unexplained by medicine. A medical condition can also be present. This diagnosis does not mean someone is pretending, and a normal test does not settle every health question.",cooccur:"Anxiety, depression, pain or other medical conditions may also be present."}},
specificphobia:{clinical:{course:"Trigger-linked fear or avoidance typically persists six months or longer. Assess developmental and cultural context, proportionality and clinical significance.",impact:"Assess restricted activities, access to medical care or travel, and distress; dislike alone is insufficient.",exclusions:"Distinguish actual danger, PTSD reminders, OCD concerns, social scrutiny and agoraphobic escape/help fears.",cooccur:"Other phobias, anxiety disorders and depression may coexist. A triggered panic attack does not by itself establish panic disorder."},plain:{course:"Fear or avoidance tied to a particular trigger usually lasts at least six months. Consider age, culture, real danger and the effect on life.",impact:"Ask about activities, medical care or travel the person misses, and distress. Simply disliking something is not enough.",exclusions:"Ask whether the fear is about real danger, trauma reminders, OCD, other people's judgment or being unable to leave or get help.",cooccur:"Other fears, anxiety conditions or depression may also be present. A panic attack at the feared trigger alone does not mean panic disorder."}},
agoraphobia:{clinical:{course:"Fear involves at least two characteristic situation categories and typically persists six months or longer, with escape/help concerns, avoidance or distressed endurance.",impact:"Assess mobility, access, accompaniment needs, work, relationships and distress in context.",exclusions:"Distinguish realistic access or safety barriers, social scrutiny, specific phobia, trauma reminders and medical limitations.",cooccur:"Panic disorder and other anxiety or depressive disorders may coexist. Diagnose panic and agoraphobia separately when both are supported."},plain:{course:"Fear affects at least two kinds of situations, such as transport, crowds, open spaces, enclosed places or going out alone. It usually lasts at least six months and centers on escape or help being difficult.",impact:"Ask about getting around, access, needing someone along, work, relationships and distress.",exclusions:"Real access or safety barriers, fear of judgment, a particular trigger, trauma reminders or physical limitations may explain avoidance differently.",cooccur:"Panic disorder, other anxiety conditions or depression may also be present. Panic disorder and agoraphobia each need their own assessment."}},
cyclothymic:{clinical:{course:"Numerous subthreshold hypomanic and depressive periods persist at least two years in adults or one year in youth, present at least half the time with no symptom-free interval beyond two months. Full mood episodes have never been met.",impact:"Assess distress and cumulative relational, educational or occupational impairment rather than assuming a harmless temperament.",exclusions:"Assess substances, medications, medical illness and primary psychosis. Full mania, hypomania or major depression changes the diagnostic evaluation.",cooccur:"Anxiety and substance problems may coexist. Longitudinal records help distinguish chronic variability from brief situational reactivity."},plain:{course:"Many periods of higher and lower mood fall short of full episodes for at least two years in adults or one year in young people. Symptoms are present at least half the time, with no break longer than two months. There has never been a full manic, hypomanic or major depressive episode.",impact:"Ask about distress and effects over time on relationships, school or work. Do not assume the pattern is simply personality.",exclusions:"Check substances, medicines, health problems and psychotic conditions. A full mood episode changes which diagnosis should be considered.",cooccur:"Anxiety or substance problems can also be present. A timeline helps distinguish long-term shifts from short reactions to events."}}
};

/* Each entry follows the a/b order in the authored clinical pair. */
const plainOverlapReview=[
["adhd","gad",["Trouble concentrating","Restlessness","Difficulty finishing tasks"],"ADHD starts in childhood and affects attention or impulse control in several settings.","GAD centers on hard-to-control worry about several parts of life.","Were attention difficulties present before worry became a problem? Does attention improve when worry eases?"],
["asd","social",["Discomfort with social situations","Social interaction can take effort","Avoiding social situations"],"Autism involves a lifelong pattern of communication and relationships, together with repeated patterns, routines, interests or sensory experiences.","Social anxiety centers on fear of being judged or watched by other people.","What were communication, play, routines and sensory experiences like early in life? What does the person fear will happen with others?"],
["ocd","ocpd",["Repeated patterns or rigidity","Attention to order or details","Daily life or relationships can be affected"],"OCD involves thoughts or rituals that take substantial time or cause distress or difficulties; rituals may happen entirely in the mind.","OCPD involves a lasting, broad pattern of strict standards, perfectionism and control that makes adapting or working with others harder.","What happens if a ritual or rule cannot be followed? Are there upsetting thoughts, repeated mental steps, or standards that make tasks and relationships difficult?"],
["mdd","bipolar2",["Depressive episodes may look the same","Changes in sleep, energy or concentration","Loss of interest or difficulty functioning"],"MDD involves depressive episodes without a history of mania or hypomania that establishes a bipolar course.","Bipolar II involves depression and past hypomania: a distinct, noticeable period of increased mood and energy, without mania.","Has there been a distinct period of at least four consecutive days of unusually increased energy or activity with mood changes and other symptoms? Was it a clear change from usual, observable by others? Has there ever been mania?"],
["bipolar1","bipolar2",["Distinct changes in mood and energy","Changes in sleep need and activity","Depressive episodes may occur"],"Bipolar I requires at least one manic episode. A depressive episode is not required.","Bipolar II requires depression and hypomania, without any past mania. It can still seriously affect life.","Was there ever a high-energy mood episode with major impairment, need for hospital care, or loss of contact with reality?"],
["ptsd","gad",["Sleep or concentration difficulties","Feeling on edge or worried","Avoidance or apprehension"],"PTSD follows an event that meets the trauma requirement, with a particular pattern of unwanted reminders, avoidance and other changes.","GAD involves hard-to-control worry across several parts of life, whether or not trauma has occurred.","Which symptoms are tied to trauma reminders? Does worry cover many unrelated parts of life?"],
["bpd","bipolar2",["Emotional shifts or depression","Impulsive actions may occur","Substantial emotional distress"],"BPD involves a lasting pattern in relationships, sense of self and emotions across situations.","Bipolar II involves distinct periods of depression and hypomania with a change in mood and energy.","How long do changes last? What happens to sleep need and energy? How do relationship events connect to the changes?"],
["panic","gad",["Anxiety and body sensations","Worry about what might happen","Difficulties in everyday life"],"Panic disorder involves repeated unexpected attacks followed by worry about more attacks or changes in behavior.","GAD centers on continuing worry about several parts of life.","Do attacks arrive unexpectedly? Is ongoing worry mainly about attacks, or many other concerns?"],
["adhd","asd",["Difficulties planning or finishing tasks","Challenges at school, work or with others","Sensory or regulation difficulties may occur"],"ADHD centers on attention and/or activity and impulse control that affect daily life.","Autism centers on communication and relationships together with repeated patterns, routines, interests or sensory experiences.","Which patterns were present early in life? What makes each task hard, and what support helps? Are both patterns present?"],
["mdd","pdd",["Low mood","Changes in energy or concentration","Daily life can be affected"],"MDD involves a full depressive episode. Episodes may be single, repeated or long-lasting.","PDD involves a longstanding low-mood pattern over years, sometimes including full depressive episodes.","What was mood like over the past several years? Were there breaks, and periods of more intense symptoms?"],
["schizo","bipolar1",["Voices or fixed false beliefs may occur","Thinking or behavior may become disorganized","Daily functioning can change"],"In schizophrenia, voices, fixed false beliefs or other psychotic symptoms are not limited to periods of very low or high mood. Full mood episodes take up less than half of the illness.","In bipolar I with psychotic features, loss of contact with reality occurs during mood episodes, and there is a history of mania.","When did voices or fixed beliefs occur compared with mood episodes? How much of the illness involved full mood episodes?"],
["schizo","mdd",["Loss of contact with reality may occur","Daily functioning can decline","Withdrawal or reduced motivation"],"Schizophrenia can involve voices, fixed false beliefs or disorganized thinking outside depression. Reduced expression or motivation can resemble depression.","In MDD with psychotic features, voices or fixed false beliefs occur only within depressive episodes.","Did these experiences ever occur without a depressive episode? What changed in interest, mood, expression and motivation?"],
["schizo","bipolar2",["Depression or reduced motivation","Daily functioning can be affected","Voices or fixed false beliefs may occur, depending on the episode"],"Schizophrenia involves an ongoing pattern of voices, fixed false beliefs or disorganized thinking or behavior that is not limited to mood episodes.","Bipolar II can include psychosis during depression. Hypomania does not include psychosis; psychosis during a high-energy episode points to mania.","Did psychotic experiences happen only during depression, during elevated mood, or outside mood episodes? Was there ever mania?"],
["bipolar1","bpd",["Impulsive behavior","Intense emotions","Relationship difficulties"],"Bipolar I involves distinct manic episodes with a clear increase in energy and activity from the person's usual state.","BPD involves a lasting pattern of difficulties with relationships, sense of self and emotions, sometimes strongly affected by interpersonal events.","Chart mood, sleep need, energy and functioning over time. Do changes form sustained episodes, follow relationship events, or both?"],
["ptsd","bpd",["Difficulty managing strong emotions","Relationship strain","Feeling disconnected or under threat"],"PTSD requires a qualifying traumatic event and a particular pattern of trauma-linked symptoms.","BPD involves broad, lasting difficulties with relationships, identity and emotions, often including sensitivity to abandonment.","Assess trauma-linked symptoms and longstanding relationship and self-image patterns separately. What was present before and after the event?"],
["ptsd","adjustment",["Distress after stressful experiences","Changes in sleep or concentration","Avoidance or feeling on edge"],"PTSD requires an event that meets the trauma requirement and a particular pattern of symptoms lasting more than a month.","Adjustment disorder follows an identifiable stressor, which need not meet the trauma requirement, and is used when another disorder does not better explain symptoms.","What happened, when did symptoms begin, and are the required trauma-linked patterns present? Does another diagnosis better fit?"],
["mdd","adjustment",["Low mood","Sleep or appetite changes","Difficulties in daily life"],"MDD involves a full depressive episode, even if it began after a stressful event.","Adjustment disorder describes stress-linked difficulties that another disorder does not better explain.","Is there a full depressive episode, including duration, other symptoms and daily-life impact? How do symptoms relate to the stressor?"],
["gad","adjustment",["Worry or tension","Poor sleep","Trouble concentrating"],"GAD involves hard-to-control worry across several areas on most days for at least six months.","Adjustment disorder is linked to a particular stressor and begins within three months of it.","Does worry extend beyond this stressor? Did it exist earlier, and how long has it lasted?"],
["gad","ocd",["Repeated distressing thoughts","Asking for reassurance","Trouble shifting attention"],"GAD centers on continuing worry about several real-life concerns.","OCD involves intrusive thoughts and/or repeated actions or mental steps with substantial time cost, distress or daily-life impact.","What is the person thinking about? Do they check, repeat, neutralize a thought or use mental rituals? What happens if they stop?"],
["ocd","illness",["Repeated checking","Seeking reassurance","Persistent fear"],"OCD may involve intrusive illness fears and repeated actions or mental steps intended to neutralize them.","Illness anxiety centers on having or getting a serious illness when physical symptoms are absent or mild.","What illness or outcome is feared? What does checking aim to prevent? Are there other intrusive thoughts or rituals?"],
["illness","somatic",["Health-related anxiety","Much attention to health","Daily life can be affected"],"Illness anxiety centers on fear of serious illness, with absent or mild physical symptoms.","Somatic symptom disorder includes distressing physical symptoms and excessive worry or behavior related to them.","How prominent are physical symptoms? How do both symptoms and worry affect the day? What medical assessment is needed?"],
["panic","agoraphobia",["Avoidance","Fear of panic-like sensations","Worry before entering situations"],"Panic disorder requires repeated unexpected attacks and continuing concern or behavior change afterward.","Agoraphobia involves fear in at least two kinds of situations because leaving or getting help may feel difficult. Panic disorder is not required.","Are attacks unexpected? Which places are feared, and why? Assess the attack pattern and the place-related fear separately."],
["social","specificphobia",["Avoidance","Intense fear in particular situations","Physical anxiety symptoms"],"Social anxiety centers on being watched, embarrassed or judged negatively.","Specific phobia centers on a particular object or situation, such as needles or flying, rather than mainly on judgment by others.","What is the feared outcome: other people's judgment, or danger from the particular trigger?"],
["adhd","mdd",["Concentration problems","Difficulty getting started","Trouble finishing tasks"],"ADHD involves childhood-onset attention or impulse-control difficulties across settings, before the current depressive episode.","Depression can cause new or worsened attention problems alongside low mood or loss of interest and other changes.","What was attention like in childhood and before depression? When did motivation, interest, sleep and mood change?"],
["adhd","bipolar1",["Distractibility","Increased activity","Impulsive behavior"],"ADHD involves a longstanding developmental pattern that appears across settings.","Bipolar I involves distinct mania with increased mood and energy, a change from baseline and substantial effects on functioning.","Was there a sustained change in energy, sleep need, self-confidence and behavior? Were similar attention difficulties present in childhood?"],
["adhd","insomnia",["Poor attention","Restlessness","Difficulty planning or organizing"],"ADHD starts in childhood; the attention or impulse-control pattern is not limited to periods of poor sleep.","Insomnia involves persistent sleep difficulty despite enough opportunity, with daytime effects.","Did attention problems begin before sleep difficulties? Is there enough time and a suitable setting for sleep?"],
["mdd","insomnia",["Sleep disturbance","Fatigue","Poor concentration"],"MDD includes low mood or loss of interest with other changes forming a depressive episode.","Insomnia involves persistent sleep difficulty and daytime effects. It may need its own care even when depression is also present.","When did sleep, mood and interest change? Does each pattern meet its own requirements?"],
["anorexia","bulimia",["Weight or shape concerns","Restriction may occur","Bingeing or purging may occur"],"Anorexia involves significantly low weight caused by restriction, alongside the relevant fears, behavior and body-related difficulties. Some presentations include bingeing or purging.","Bulimia involves repeated binges and harmful attempts to make up for eating; do not assign it when this pattern occurs only during anorexia.","Assess nutrition, growth or weight changes, restriction, loss of control, attempts to make up for eating and current medical safety."],
["bulimia","bed",["Binges with loss of control","Distress or shame","Eating in secret may occur"],"Bulimia includes regular harmful attempts to compensate for binges, and strong influence of weight or shape on self-worth.","Binge-eating disorder includes marked distress about binges without regular harmful compensation.","Ask directly and respectfully about vomiting, fasting, laxative misuse and excessive exercise, as well as binge frequency and distress."],
["aud","cannabis",["Craving or trouble controlling use","Difficulties with responsibilities or relationships","Tolerance or withdrawal may occur"],"Alcohol use disorder involves alcohol-related impaired control or consequences. Severe alcohol withdrawal can be medically dangerous.","Cannabis use disorder involves cannabis-related impaired control or consequences. Effects vary with dose, strength and route.","Assess each substance separately: use pattern, attempts to cut back, consequences, withdrawal, co-use and when other symptoms occur."],
["cannabis","schizo",["Suspiciousness or unusual perceptions may occur around cannabis use","Thinking may become disorganized","Daily functioning may change"],"Cannabis use disorder concerns control over use and its consequences. Cannabis-related psychosis is a separate assessment and can occur without a use disorder.","Schizophrenia involves an ongoing pattern of voices, fixed false beliefs or disorganized thinking or behavior, a decline in daily functioning, and duration requirements. Substance effects do not explain the pattern.","Compare cannabis use, intoxication, time without use and symptoms such as voices or fixed false beliefs. Were those symptoms present before use or during a sustained period without use?" ]
];

const pairCooccurrenceReview={
"adhd|gad":["ADHD and GAD can coexist; establish developmental attention symptoms and an independent worry syndrome.","A person can have both. Assess childhood attention difficulties and the worry pattern separately."],
"asd|social":["Autism and social anxiety may coexist. Social distress does not determine which explanation is present.","An autistic person may also fear judgment. Ask about lifelong communication patterns and current fears separately."],
"ocd|ocpd":["OCD and OCPD can coexist; evaluate the syndrome and pervasive personality pattern independently.","A person can have both OCD and OCPD. Assess thoughts or rituals separately from broad, longstanding rigid standards."],
"bipolar2|mdd":["Do not add MDD to bipolar II to label its depressive episodes; evaluate the lifetime bipolar course.","Depressive episodes are part of bipolar II. They are not separately labeled MDD when the bipolar course explains them."],
"bipolar1|bipolar2":["Bipolar I and II are alternative classifications; a lifetime manic episode changes the classification to bipolar I.","These are alternative diagnoses. A past manic episode means bipolar I should be considered rather than bipolar II."],
"gad|ptsd":["Both may be diagnosed when a broad worry syndrome is independently supported beyond trauma-related symptoms.","Both can occur if broad worry has its own pattern beyond symptoms tied to trauma."],
"bipolar2|bpd":["Both may coexist; establish enduring personality patterns outside mood episodes.","Both can occur. Ask about lasting relationship and identity patterns when mood episodes have settled."],
"gad|panic":["Panic disorder and GAD can coexist; distinguish attack-related concern from independent multi-domain worry.","Both can occur. Separate worry about attacks from a wider pattern of ongoing worry."],
"adhd|asd":["Both developmental diagnoses may coexist; neither should be inferred from executive difficulty alone.","Both can occur. Problems planning or finishing tasks alone do not establish either diagnosis."],
"mdd|pdd":["Persistent depression may include full major depressive episodes. Use the appropriate course/specifiers and current coding guidance rather than assume unrelated syndromes.","Full depressive episodes can occur within years of low mood. Describe the full timeline and check the appropriate diagnosis and code."],
"bipolar1|schizo":["Do not simply combine schizophrenia and bipolar I because psychosis and mood symptoms coexist. Evaluate schizoaffective disorder and the proportion of illness occupied by major mood episodes.","Psychosis plus mania does not automatically mean two diagnoses. Consider schizoaffective disorder and how mood episodes relate to the whole illness."],
"mdd|schizo":["Depression can coexist with schizophrenia, but assess negative symptoms and schizoaffective disorder before assigning an independent depressive diagnosis.","Depression can occur with schizophrenia. Check whether reduced motivation reflects depression, psychotic illness or both, and consider schizoaffective disorder."],
"bipolar2|schizo":["Evaluate schizoaffective and other psychotic disorders before stacking labels. Psychosis confined to depression can occur in bipolar II; elevated-mood psychosis indicates mania.","Check the whole timeline before combining labels. Bipolar II can include psychosis during depression; psychosis in a high-energy episode points to mania."],
"bipolar1|bpd":["Both may coexist when mania and the enduring personality pattern are independently established.","Both can occur if there is evidence for mania and for a separate, lasting relationship and identity pattern."],
"bpd|ptsd":["Both may coexist; neither trauma history nor self-harm alone establishes either diagnosis.","Both can occur. Trauma history or self-harm alone does not establish either condition."],
"adjustment|ptsd":["Do not use adjustment disorder for symptoms better explained by PTSD. A distinct response to another stressor requires its own assessment.","Use PTSD when it better explains the symptoms. A separate response to another stressor needs its own assessment."],
"adjustment|mdd":["Adjustment disorder is not a substitute or add-on for the same symptoms when full MDD better explains them.","If a full depressive episode explains the symptoms, adjustment disorder is not added for those same symptoms."],
"adjustment|gad":["Do not attribute the same worry syndrome to adjustment disorder when GAD better accounts for it.","If GAD explains the worry pattern, do not also label that same pattern adjustment disorder."],
"gad|ocd":["Both can coexist; separate independent generalized worry from obsessions and compulsions.","Both can occur. Separate broad worry from intrusive thoughts or rituals that have their own pattern."],
"illness|ocd":["Differentiate health-focused obsessions from illness preoccupation; avoid assigning two diagnoses to the same syndrome without independent evidence.","Health fears can have different explanations. Do not assign two diagnoses to the same pattern without evidence for each."],
"illness|somatic":["These are usually alternative formulations of the same health-focused presentation; symptom prominence and the full clinical picture guide selection.","These usually offer different explanations of the same health-related pattern. Physical symptom burden helps distinguish them."],
"agoraphobia|panic":["Both can coexist and are diagnosed separately when each is supported; neither requires the other.","Both can occur, and each needs its own assessment. Either can also occur without the other."],
"social|specificphobia":["Both can coexist when there are distinct social-evaluative and object/situation-specific fears.","Both can occur if fear of judgment and a separate trigger-related fear each have their own pattern."],
"adhd|mdd":["Depression can coexist with ADHD; assess baseline developmental symptoms separately from episode-related worsening.","Both can occur. Compare childhood and usual attention with changes during depression."],
"adhd|bipolar1":["Both can coexist; assess ADHD outside mood episodes and establish true mania rather than continuous activity alone.","Both can occur. Assess usual attention outside mood episodes and establish a real manic episode."],
"adhd|insomnia":["Insomnia may worsen ADHD and coexist independently; assess other sleep disorders before attributing all daytime symptoms to ADHD.","Poor sleep can worsen ADHD, and both can occur. Other sleep problems may also explain daytime difficulties."],
"insomnia|mdd":["Insomnia may warrant a separate diagnosis when independently clinically significant, even with depression.","Sleep difficulty may need its own diagnosis and care even when depression is also present."],
"anorexia|bulimia":["Binge/purge symptoms during anorexia are classified within anorexia; historical diagnostic crossover may occur.","Bingeing or purging can be part of anorexia. A person's eating-disorder pattern can change over time."],
"bed|bulimia":["Do not assign both for the same episode pattern. Changes in compensation over time may alter the diagnosis.","These do not describe the same binge pattern at the same time. Changes in attempts to compensate can change the diagnosis."],
"aud|cannabis":["Both use disorders can coexist; assess each substance's criteria and withdrawal risks separately.","Both use disorders can occur. Check each substance's effects, consequences and withdrawal risks separately."],
"cannabis|schizo":["CUD can coexist with schizophrenia. Cannabis exposure, cannabis-induced psychosis and CUD are not interchangeable diagnoses.","Cannabis use disorder and schizophrenia can both occur. Cannabis use, cannabis-related psychosis and a use disorder mean different things." ]
};

